 2. Structural Infrastructure-as-Code Configuration To establish this secure enterprise perimeter programmatically, future cloud administrators must configure the orchestration environment blueprint directly inside the root render.yaml specification file [13].Replace or wrap your current execution parameters using the strict group cluster schema below:

# =============================================================
# Render Private Space Project Specification
# =============================================================
projects:
  - name: enterprise-finops-suite
    environments:
      - name: corporate-production
        
        # 1. Activate strict private cloud firewall parameters
        networking:
          isolation: enabled # ◄── Drops all unauthenticated public internet traffic [13]
        
        # 2. Bind the dockerized cost utility into the isolated workspace
        services:
          - type: web
            name: azure-cost-dashboard
            runtime: docker
            dockerfilePath: ./Dockerfile
            plan: free
            healthCheckPath: /api/health
            envVars:
              - key: NODE_ENV
                value: production
#
3. Render Private Space Activation Steps
The isolation flag only applies after a Private Space has been created. Follow this sequence:
1. In the Render dashboard, click New + and select Private Space.
2. Choose the region closest to your corporate office and create the space.
3. Once created, go to the dashboard view for your services.
4. Click Settings > Networking and set Isolation to Enabled.
5. Under Inbound Rules, add your corporate VPN public IP ranges.
6. Redeploy the service — all public inbound traffic is now dropped.

#
4. Azure VNet Integration for the Serverless Backend
The Azure Functions backend (GetCloudCosts) must be locked behind a virtual network so it is unreachable from the public internet.

#
PowerShell
# 1. Create a dedicated VNet for the FinOps backend
az network vnet create --resource-group FinOps-Backend-RG --name finops-vnet --address-prefix 10.10.0.0/16 --subnet-name backend-subnet --subnet-prefix 10.10.1.0/24

# 2. Create a private endpoint for the storage account used by the Function App
az network private-endpoint create --resource-group FinOps-Backend-RG --name finops-storage-pe --vnet-name finops-vnet --subnet backend-subnet --private-connection-resource-id "/subscriptions/<your-subscription-id>/resourceGroups/FinOps-Backend-RG/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/<storage-account>" --group-id table --connection-name finops-storage-link

# 3. Integrate the Function App into the VNet
az functionapp vnet-integration add --name <app-name> --resource-group FinOps-Backend-RG --vnet finops-vnet --subnet backend-subnet

# 4. Lock down the Function App to only accept traffic from the VNet
az functionapp config access-restriction add --resource-group FinOps-Backend-RG --name <app-name> --rule-name deny-public --ip-address any --action Deny --priority 100
#
5. Verify Both Layers Are Isolated
After applying both Render and Azure isolation, run this verification sequence from a machine outside the corporate network:
1. Try to reach the frontend URL in a browser — you should get a connection timeout.
2. Try to curl the backend API: curl -I https://<app-name>.azurewebsites.net/api/GetCloudCosts — you should get a 403 Forbidden or connection refused.
3. Connect to your corporate VPN, then retry both — they should respond normally.
#
Common Error: Function App VNet Integration Fails
If az functionapp vnet-integration add returns a SubnetNotFound error, the subnet name may differ from what was created. Run az network vnet subnet list --resource-group FinOps-Backend-RG --vnet-name finops-vnet to confirm the exact name.
